{
  "verdict": "faithful_with_caveats",
  "divergences": [
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Omission of arithmetic, equality, and general terms: unlike Reinhardt (P-R323, P-R323b) and Koellner (P-K353), Def11 omits the axioms of Peano Arithmetic (including induction over L_EA) and Leibniz's law for equality, and restricts universal instantiation (`inst`) to variables rather than arbitrary terms `t`. This is inherited from the language fragment and is explicitly declared separately in the author's claim."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Unrestricted hypothesis set `T`: P-R323b defines `T ⊢ p` only when `T` is a set of sentences, whereas `Def15 (T : Set Form)` allows `T` to contain open formulas (as noted in the claim). Because every axiom in `Def14` is required to be a sentence (`Def13`), `Def15` agrees with the source on sets of sentences, but cannot apply any logical axioms (not even propositional tautologies) directly to open hypotheses in `T`."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Packaging of non-logical axioms in `Def15`: P-R323b describes `T ⊢ p` as deducibility by modus ponens from logical axioms and sentences of `T` (treating `Σ ∪ KΣ` as a theory `T`, as in P-K353), whereas `Def15` builds Reinhardt's four `K`-schemas and `KΣ` directly into its base `ax` rule alongside the logical axioms, making `T` a set of extra hypotheses on top of the epistemic logic."
    },
    {
      "severity": "minor",
      "description": "Vacuous quantifiers in universal closures: `Def12` permits prefixing arbitrary universal quantifiers (including vacuous or repeated ones) to a schema instance before checking closedness (`Def13`), rather than binding only the free variables of the formula. This is deductively equivalent given `vac`, `dist`, and `k2`."
    }
  ],
  "summary": "Within the declared pure-epistemic first-order fragment without arithmetic, equality, or non-variable terms, `Def9`–`Def15` faithfully formalize Reinhardt's and Koellner's proof system (classical tautologies, Enderton-style FOL schemas, Reinhardt's four `K`-axioms, closed universal closures with optional single `K`-prefixing `Σ ∪ KΣ`, and modus ponens, with necessitation admissible for `T = ∅`). Only minor differences remain: the declared omission of PA/equality/terms, allowing open formulas in `T` (which are inert with respect to closed axioms), building the `K`-axioms into `Def15`, and allowing vacuous quantifiers in universal closures."
}
